  "fixture_note": "Synthetic capture. No real profile is read here: every record is a hand-built clone of the shape probes/browser_prefs.ps1 emits, sized so that each browser rule fires on a documented subject and one rule (BR-01 on a plain-only document) can only fire after the anchor fallback fix. tools/selftest.ps1 sections [1]-[5] treat this file as the oracle. Values are fake; the domain strings are the blocklisted ones on purpose. perm_grants is the merged content-settings ledger as setting|origin|last_written_local strings: only the plain Preferences document carries it on a real machine, so BR-08 can fire on a profile whose startup page is clean. The search provider block is scoped the same way it is in Chromium - default_search_provider_data only ever lives in Secure Preferences - so Chrome/Default carries the policy-enforced case, Edge/Profile 2 the blocklisted-engine case, and the three records whose engine is unreadable (Edge/Profile 1, Edge/Default hashed by Edge itself, Chrome/Profile 3 with no Secure Preferences at all) are what BR-10 reports as blind spots rather than as clean. shortcuts is the machine-level .lnk ledger the same probe appends: three rows, one of which points a browser at a blocklisted startup URL (what a hijacker leaves when it replaces the Desktop shortcut rather than the prefs file), one of which is a plain browser shortcut with no URL at all, and one of which is a legitimately URL-bearing PWA launcher (msedge_proxy --app-url). The last two must produce no finding, so BR-11 is pinned in both directions on the fixture instead of only on an infected box. launch_cmds is the registry launch chain (StartMenuInternet plus the ProgIDs that http/https and the browser document types resolve to) and file_assocs is the browser document-type handler table. These two arrays carry a deliberate asymmetry with shortcuts: BR-11 needs the blocklist because a .lnk that opens a site is a normal Windows construct (a PWA launcher), while BR-12 fires on any literal URL because a real-machine sweep of every browser-named open command in both Classes hives found 33 of 33 with none, so the URL itself is the signal - which is why one fixture row carries a benign Outlook app-url and is expected to fire, with the advice telling the reader how to judge it. file_assocs exists to pin the false-positive trap that the scope was narrowed for: on a real machine 13 of 211 UserChoice extensions point at a ProgID that does not resolve under Classes, and they are AppX packages and Windows components, so the AppX-shaped row must NOT fire while the two genuinely dangling ones must, one with a Hash (written through the proper API) and one without (hand-imported). policy_entries rows are synthetic too: two forcing policies, two benign enterprise policies that must stay silent, one capability grant, and two empty policy keys split across hives so only the user-hive one is a finding.",
